22:50:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you’d make that connection. You see water droplets form instantly on your glasses, so it seems impossible that the same thing isn’t happening *inside* your throat and lungs. That’s a really logical observation, and it deserves a careful, honest answer. Let’s walk through why what happens on your glasses is very different from what happens inside your airway. --- ### 1. The key difference: temperature and surface material Condensation happens when warm, moist air touches a surface that is **cooler than the dew point**. - Your **glasses** have been sitting in room air, so they’re probably around **20–22 °C** (or cooler). - The **inside of your throat and windpipe** is lined with living tissue that’s supplied with blood and kept at a steady **37 °C** — core body temperature. Steam in a shower is typically around **38–42 °C**. Its dew point is very close to that temperature because the air is nearly saturated with water vapour. - When that air hits your 20 °C glasses, the surface is **way below the dew point**, so water condenses out rapidly, forming visible droplets. - When the same air enters your throat, the surface is already **37 °C** — just a degree or two below the steam temperature. The temperature difference is tiny, so the driving force for condensation is tiny. Only a microscopic film of moisture forms, not visible droplets that could pool and block the airway. ### 2. That microscopic film isn’t a choking hazard Even if we ignore temperature and assume *some* condensation does occur, the amount is still minuscule. As I mentioned earlier, a single breath’s worth of saturated air cooling from 40 °C to 37 °C releases only about **3–4 milligrams of water** — far less than a single drop. Your trachea (windpipe) is about 2 cm wide, lined with a mucus layer that normally covers the walls. That tiny bit of extra moisture simply joins the mucus film. It never accumulates to form a liquid blockage. To physically c